---
// The four steps of "How it works" in Lenticulum.jl's README, each with a small looping drawing.
// The drawings start when the step scrolls into view (the page's .reveal → .in).
---

<ol class="hiw">
  <li class="step reveal">
    <div class="art" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 220 150">
        <path class="contour c2" d="M40 92 C 40 30, 160 10, 182 62 C 200 104, 130 140, 88 128 C 56 119, 40 112, 40 92 Z"></path>
        <path class="contour c1" d="M58 90 C 58 46, 150 32, 166 66 C 180 96, 128 122, 92 114 C 68 108, 58 104, 58 90 Z"></path>
        <path class="zero" pathLength="1" d="M75 88 C 75 62, 140 54, 150 70 C 160 88, 124 104, 98 100 C 82 97, 75 96, 75 88 Z"></path>
        <text x="152" y="118" class="lbl">r<tspan dy="3" font-size="9">θ</tspan><tspan dy="-3">(z) = 0</tspan></text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <span class="num mono">01</span>
    <h3>A relation is the zero set of a learned residual</h3>
    <p>
      Instead of a function <em>y</em> = <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>), the model learns a residual
      <em>r</em><sub>θ</sub>(<em>z</em>): one function of all the coordinates together,
      <em>z</em> = (<em>x</em>, <em>y</em>, …), that is zero on the data and nonzero away from
      it. The relation is where it vanishes:
      <em>R</em><sub>θ</sub> = {"{"} z : <em>r</em><sub>θ</sub>(z) = 0 {"}"}. The circle
      <em>x</em>² + <em>y</em>² − 1 = 0 is the simplest example.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li class="step reveal" style="--d:.06s">
    <div class="art" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 220 150">
        <g class="chip chip-a">
          <rect x="22" y="40" width="52" height="52" rx="8"></rect>
          <text x="48" y="73" class="sym">x</text>
        </g>
        <g class="chip chip-b">
          <rect x="84" y="40" width="52" height="52" rx="8"></rect>
          <text x="110" y="73" class="sym">y</text>
        </g>
        <g class="chip chip-u">
          <rect x="146" y="40" width="52" height="52" rx="8"></rect>
          <text x="172" y="73" class="sym">u</text>
        </g>
        <text x="48" y="114" class="tag tag-a1">input ∞</text>
        <text x="48" y="114" class="tag tag-a2">output 0</text>
        <text x="110" y="114" class="tag tag-b1">output 0</text>
        <text x="110" y="114" class="tag tag-b2">input ∞</text>
        <text x="172" y="114" class="tag">latent</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <span class="num mono">02</span>
    <h3>A query chooses a polarity</h3>
    <p>
      Each query sorts the coordinates into inputs (known), outputs (wanted) and latents (neither);
      this choice is called a polarity. Each coordinate gets a precision, one over its variance:
      ∞ holds it fixed, 0 leaves it free, anything in between is a noisy measurement. Asked the
      other way round, the same model swaps inputs and outputs.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li class="step reveal" style="--d:.12s">
    <div class="art" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 220 150">
        <path class="zero static" d="M20 112 C 70 112, 100 40, 200 34"></path>
        <line class="clampline" x1="150" y1="10" x2="150" y2="140"></line>
        <path class="newton" pathLength="1" d="M150 124 L150 70 L150 50 L150 44.5"></path>
        <circle class="start" cx="150" cy="124" r="4.5"></circle>
        <circle class="it it1" cx="150" cy="70" r="3"></circle>
        <circle class="it it2" cx="150" cy="50" r="3"></circle>
        <circle class="root" cx="150" cy="44.5" r="6"></circle>
        <text x="160" y="128" class="tag left">start</text>
        <text x="122" y="30" class="tag left">root</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <span class="num mono">03</span>
    <h3>Inference is root finding</h3>
    <p>
      With the inputs held fixed, a solver such as Newton's method adjusts the free coordinates
      until the residual is zero, the way a deep equilibrium model is evaluated. Several roots
      mean several answers: one <em>x</em> on the circle has two <em>y</em>.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li class="step reveal" style="--d:.18s">
    <div class="art" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 220 150">
        <g class="unrolled">
          <rect x="16" y="20" width="18" height="18" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect x="44" y="20" width="18" height="18" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect x="72" y="20" width="18" height="18" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect x="100" y="20" width="18" height="18" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect x="128" y="20" width="18" height="18" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect x="156" y="20" width="18" height="18" rx="3"></rect>
          <line x1="10" y1="29" x2="182" y2="29" class="strike"></line>
          <text x="196" y="33" class="tag">unrolled</text>
        </g>
        <circle class="theta" cx="36" cy="112" r="15"></circle>
        <text x="36" y="117" class="sym small">θ</text>
        <circle class="answer" cx="176" cy="112" r="15"></circle>
        <text x="176" y="117" class="sym small inv">z*</text>
        <path id="ift-arc" class="arc" d="M160 104 C 130 64, 82 64, 52 104"></path>
        <circle class="pulse" r="4">
          <animateMotion dur="2.2s" repeatCount="indefinite" path="M160 104 C 130 64, 82 64, 52 104"></animateMotion>
        </circle>
        <text x="106" y="64" class="tag">one linear solve</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <span class="num mono">04</span>
    <h3>Backpropagation is the implicit function theorem</h3>
    <p>
      Training needs the gradient of the answer. The implicit function theorem gives it from one
      linear solve at the answer, for the parameters, the inputs and the precisions alike. The
      solver's steps are not unrolled (stored and differentiated through), so memory does not
      grow with them.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>

<style>
  .hiw {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--line);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .hiw {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 1120px) {
    .hiw {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    }
  }
  .step {
    background: var(--bg);
    padding: 24px 24px 32px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  .art {
    height: 150px;
    margin: 0 -8px 18px;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .art svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    overflow: visible;
  }
  .num {
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .step h3 {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
  .step p {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 15px;
  }
  .step em {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.1em;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  /* shared drawing styles (zero specificity, so a class below can fill a shape) */
  :where(svg) :where(path, line, rect, circle) {
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 1.4;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }
  .lbl,
  .sym {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    fill: currentColor;
    stroke: none;
  }
  .lbl {
    font-size: 15px;
  }
  .sym {
    font-size: 26px;
    text-anchor: middle;
  }
  .sym.small {
    font-size: 17px;
  }
  .tag {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 9.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    fill: var(--muted);
    stroke: none;
    text-anchor: middle;
  }
  .tag.left {
    text-anchor: start;
  }

  /* every animation waits until the step is in view */
  .step :is(path, line, rect, circle, text, g) {
    animation-play-state: paused;
  }
  .step.in :is(path, line, rect, circle, text, g) {
    animation-play-state: running;
  }

  /* 01: the zero set draws itself inside fading level sets */
  .contour {
    stroke-opacity: 0.18;
    stroke-dasharray: 3 4;
  }
  .zero {
    stroke-width: 2;
    stroke-dasharray: 1;
    stroke-dashoffset: 1;
    animation: draw 2.4s var(--ease) 0.3s infinite alternate;
  }
  .zero.static {
    stroke-dasharray: none;
    stroke-dashoffset: 0;
    animation: none;
  }
  .c1 {
    animation: breathe 3.2s ease-in-out infinite;
  }
  .c2 {
    animation: breathe 3.2s ease-in-out 0.8s infinite;
  }

  /* 02: the polarity swaps: x and y trade input and output */
  .chip rect {
    stroke-width: 1.4;
  }
  .chip-u rect {
    stroke-dasharray: 4 4;
    stroke-opacity: 0.6;
  }
  .chip-a rect,
  .chip-b rect {
    transition: fill 0.3s;
  }
  .chip-a rect {
    animation: in-out 4.8s steps(1) infinite;
  }
  .chip-b rect {
    animation: out-in 4.8s steps(1) infinite;
  }
  .chip-a text {
    animation: in-out-text 4.8s steps(1) infinite;
  }
  .chip-b text {
    animation: out-in-text 4.8s steps(1) infinite;
  }
  .tag-a1,
  .tag-b1 {
    animation: show-first 4.8s steps(1) infinite;
  }
  .tag-a2,
  .tag-b2 {
    animation: show-second 4.8s steps(1) infinite;
  }

  /* 03: Newton steps along the clamp line down to the root */
  .clampline {
    stroke-dasharray: 4 4;
    stroke-opacity: 0.55;
  }
  .newton {
    stroke-width: 1.6;
    stroke-dasharray: 1;
    stroke-dashoffset: 1;
    animation: draw-hold 3.6s var(--ease) infinite;
  }
  .start {
    fill: var(--bg);
  }
  .it {
    fill: currentColor;
    opacity: 0;
  }
  .it1 {
    animation: appear 3.6s infinite;
    animation-delay: 0s;
  }
  .it2 {
    animation: appear2 3.6s infinite;
  }
  .root {
    fill: currentColor;
    opacity: 0;
    animation: appear3 3.6s infinite;
  }

  /* 04: unrolled steps struck through; one solve carries the gradient back */
  .unrolled rect {
    stroke-opacity: 0.35;
  }
  .strike {
    stroke-opacity: 0.6;
  }
  .unrolled .tag {
    text-anchor: start;
    font-size: 9px;
  }
  .unrolled text {
    transform: translateX(-12px);
  }
  .answer {
    fill: currentColor;
  }
  .inv {
    fill: var(--bg);
  }
  .arc {
    stroke-dasharray: 3 4;
    stroke-opacity: 0.7;
  }
  .pulse {
    fill: currentColor;
    stroke: none;
  }

  @keyframes draw {
    to {
      stroke-dashoffset: 0;
    }
  }
  @keyframes draw-hold {
    0% {
      stroke-dashoffset: 1;
    }
    55%,
    100% {
      stroke-dashoffset: 0;
    }
  }
  @keyframes breathe {
    50% {
      stroke-opacity: 0.35;
    }
  }
  @keyframes in-out {
    0% {
      fill: currentColor;
    }
    50% {
      fill: none;
    }
  }
  @keyframes out-in {
    0% {
      fill: none;
    }
    50% {
      fill: currentColor;
    }
  }
  @keyframes in-out-text {
    0% {
      fill: var(--bg);
    }
    50% {
      fill: currentColor;
    }
  }
  @keyframes out-in-text {
    0% {
      fill: currentColor;
    }
    50% {
      fill: var(--bg);
    }
  }
  @keyframes show-first {
    0% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    50% {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }
  @keyframes show-second {
    0% {
      opacity: 0;
    }
    50% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }
  @keyframes appear {
    0%,
    20% {
      opacity: 0;
    }
    24%,
    92% {
      opacity: 0.6;
    }
    100% {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }
  @keyframes appear2 {
    0%,
    34% {
      opacity: 0;
    }
    38%,
    92% {
      opacity: 0.6;
    }
    100% {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }
  @keyframes appear3 {
    0%,
    50% {
      opacity: 0;
    }
    56%,
    92% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    100% {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .zero,
    .newton {
      stroke-dashoffset: 0;
    }
    .it,
    .root {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    .pulse {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
